<article class="env-lemma" id="0GQJ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GQJ">Lemma <span data-tag="0GQJ">103.10.3</span>.</a> Let $f : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ be a flat morphism of algebraic stacks. Then $Q_\mathcal {X} \circ f^* = f^* \circ Q_\mathcal {Y}$ where $Q_\mathcal {X}$ and $Q_\mathcal {Y}$ are as in Lemma <a href="/tag/0778" data-tag="0778">103.10.1</a>. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Observe that $f^*$ preserves both $\mathit{QCoh}$ and $\textit{LQCoh}^{fbc}$, see Sheaves on Stacks, Lemma <a href="/tag/06WH" data-tag="06WH">96.11.2</a> and Proposition <a href="/tag/0771" data-tag="0771">103.8.1</a>. If $\mathcal{F}$ is in $\textit{LQCoh}^{fbc}(\mathcal{O}_\mathcal {Y})$ then $Q_\mathcal {Y}(\mathcal{F}) \to \mathcal{F}$ has parasitic kernel and cokernel by Lemma <a href="/tag/0779" data-tag="0779">103.10.2</a>. As $f$ is flat we get that $f^*Q_\mathcal {Y}(\mathcal{F}) \to f^*\mathcal{F}$ has parasitic kernel and cokernel by Lemma <a href="/tag/0774" data-tag="0774">103.9.2</a>. Thus the induced map $f^*Q_\mathcal {Y}(\mathcal{F}) \to Q_\mathcal {X}(f^*\mathcal{F})$ has parasitic kernel and cokernel and hence is an isomorphism for example by Lemma <a href="/tag/0776" data-tag="0776">103.9.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>